By [F3] and [F4], smooth boundary data yield solutions smooth up to $\partial M$. For smooth test data $h$, choose a smooth collar extension $V_h$ by [F8]. In a boundary chart, write $\operatorname{div}_g(\gamma
abla_gv)=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_i(|g|^{1/2}\gamma g^{ij}\partial_jv)$ and $\Delta_gu=|g|^{-1/2}\partial_i(|g|^{1/2}g^{ij}\partial_ju)$. Apply the Euclidean Gauss-Green identity [F5] to the coordinate fluxes; summing over the finite partition gives the Riemannian boundary flux, while artificial chart boundaries cancel and the density transforms to $dS_g$. For the conductivity equation it yields $$\langle\Lambda_\gamma f,hangle=\int_{\partial M}\gamma\,\partial_{
u_{m out}}v_f\,\overline h\,dS_g,$$ since $\operatorname{div}_g(\gamma
abla_gv_f)=0$. For the Schrödinger equation it yields $$\langle\Lambda_qf,hangle=\int_{\partial M}\partial_{
u_{m out}}u_f\,\overline h\,dS_g,$$ since $(-\Delta_g+q)u_f=0$. The extensions required for the weak pairings exist by [F6], and smooth test extensions exist by [F8].

Cancel the $1$-handle represented by $R$ with the $2$-handle $B$ using
their single transverse meridional intersection. The other normal disk is
disjoint from this cancellation neighborhood; after cancellation it is a
spanning disk for the remaining attaching circle. The transported framing is
zero, so the remaining handle is $X_0(U)$. Reversing the roles gives the same
result after cancelling $R$ with $G$. This verifies both cancellations
described in the Example. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, construct] ∎

~~~tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=2.5cm,>=stealth]
  \node (x) {$B^4\cup_R h^1\cup_B h^2_0\cup_G h^2_0$};
  \node (g) [below left of=x] {$B^4\cup_U h^2_0$};
  \node (b) [below right of=x] {$B^4\cup_U h^2_0$};
  \draw[->] (x) -- node[left,align=center] {cancel $(R,B)$;\\retain $G$} (g);
  \draw[->] (x) -- node[right,align=center] {cancel $(R,G)$;\\retain $B$} (b);
\end{tikzpicture}
~~~

The sketch shows handle incidence only: dotted $R$ denotes the $1$-handle and
subscript $0$ denotes zero framing. The proof uses the disjoint normal disks and
the single-intersection cancellation, not the sketch.

